<BaseLayout title="Flat Bed Conveyors: Slider Bed vs Roller Bed Belt Guide" description="Flat bed conveyor (flat belt conveyor) guide: slider bed vs roller bed compared on load, length, and small parts, plus belt tracking, tensioning, and belting." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-types/flat-belt-conveyors/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, articleSchema]}>
<h1>Flat Belt &amp; Slider Bed Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Flat bed conveyor is the common name for a flat belt conveyor: a continuous flat belt stretched between head and tail pulleys to carry products on its upper surface. The belt rides on either a smooth slider bed (sheet metal pan) or a roller bed (support rollers beneath the belt). Flat belt conveyors handle products of any size, shape, and bottom surface, including items too small, soft, or irregularly shaped for <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a>. They are one of the most versatile general-purpose conveyor types in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging</a> and material handling.</p>
<h2 id="bed-heading">Slider Bed vs. Roller Bed Conveyor</h2>
<p>A slider bed conveyor slides the belt over a flat metal pan, while a roller bed conveyor carries the belt on closely spaced rollers. Choose a slider bed for small, light, or unstable products that need continuous support and tight end transfers. Choose a roller bed for heavier loads and longer runs, where lower belt friction reduces drive power. Load ratings below come from one manufacturer's published spec sheets and show the typical gap between the two bed types. Other suppliers rate differently, so compare model load charts.</p>
