<h3>Slider Bed Conveyors</h3>
<p>Slider bed conveyors support the belt on a smooth metal pan (the slider bed or wear strip). The belt slides across the pan surface, driven by friction between the belt bottom and the drive pulley. The pan provides continuous support across the entire belt width, making slider beds ideal for small products that would fall between rollers and for applications requiring a flat, stable conveying surface.</p>
<p>Slider beds suit light to moderate loads. Belt-to-pan friction rises with load and length, so longer or heavier slider beds need more drive power. Load ratings vary widely by design. Hytrol's <a href="https://www.cisco-eagle.com/uploads/Hytrol-Spec-Belt/Model-SB-Specs.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Model SB slider bed spec sheet</a> rates the 12-gauge galvanized pan conveyor at a maximum of 100 lbs per linear foot at its standard 65 FPM, with load charts for bed lengths up to 100 feet. Dorner's aluminum-frame <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/products/2200-series/2200-belted-conveyor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2200 Series flat belt conveyor</a>, a small precision conveyor, lists loads up to 120 lbs (total), speeds up to 400 ft/min, and lengths from 1.5 to 24 feet. Low-friction pan surfaces and belt backings reduce drag.</p>
<h3>Roller Bed Conveyors</h3>
<p>Roller bed conveyors support the belt on carry rollers. Hytrol's <a href="https://www.cisco-eagle.com/uploads/Hytrol-Spec-Belt/Model-RB-Specs.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Model RB roller bed spec sheet</a> uses 1.9-inch rollers spaced every 6 inches as standard, with 3, 9, and 12-inch centers as options that affect capacity. The rollers turn as the belt passes over them, reducing friction compared to a slider pan. Lower friction lets the same drive move heavier loads: Hytrol rates the Model RB at a maximum of 225 lbs per linear foot, against 100 lbs for its Model SB slider bed.</p>
<p>Roller bed belt conveyors serve heavy packaging applications: cases, totes, and containers that are too heavy for slider beds. They also serve long transport runs between processes where the belt's continuous surface handles products that rollers cannot (irregular shapes, small items).</p>
<h3>Knife-Edge Transfer</h3>
<p>Slider bed conveyors can be designed with a knife-edge (thin-nose) transfer at the discharge end. The drive pulley diameter is minimized to create a sharp belt edge that transfers small products with minimal gap to the next conveyor. For example, Dorner offers a 5/8-inch nose bar on its 2200 Series rated for speeds up to 200 fpm. Smaller nose bars usually carry lower speed and load limits than the conveyor's standard pulley, so check both. Standard roller bed conveyors use larger end pulleys and cannot match a knife-edge gap. Minimum transferable product size depends on nose bar diameter, the gap to the next conveyor, and product length and stiffness. Test with your smallest product.</p>
<h2 id="tracking-heading">Belt Tracking</h2>
<p>Belt tracking keeps the belt centered on the pulleys and bed. A mistracking belt drifts to one side, rubbing against the frame, damaging the belt edge, and potentially dumping products off the side. Proper tracking is the single most important belt conveyor maintenance task.</p>
<h3>Tracking Methods</h3>
<p><strong>Tail pulley adjustment:</strong> The primary tracking method. Screw adjusters at each end of the tail pulley shaft allow the operator to angle the tail pulley axis slightly. The belt tracks toward the tight (higher-tension) side. Small screw adjustments move the belt noticeably. Adjust in small increments and let the belt run several full revolutions before adjusting again. Follow the manufacturer's manual and lockout and guarding practices. <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>, the ASME safety standard for conveyors, covers guarding of pinch points such as pulleys.</p>
<p><strong>V-guide tracking:</strong> A V-shaped guide strip on the belt underside rides in a V-groove on the pulley or slider bed. The guide physically constrains the belt to center. V-guide tracking is common on precision slider bed conveyors where belt mistracking would disrupt product positioning for labeling, inspection, or packaging operations.</p>
<p><strong>Crowned pulleys:</strong> Crowned (barrel-shaped) pulleys naturally center the belt. The belt climbs toward the highest point of the crown. Light-duty conveyors use crowned pulleys as the primary tracking mechanism. Heavy-duty conveyors pair crowned pulleys with tail-pulley adjustment so the belt holds center under load.</p>
<p><strong>Training idlers:</strong> Roller bed conveyors use self-aligning training idlers that tilt automatically when the belt drifts, steering it back to center. Training idlers mount at strategic positions along the conveyor length and provide automatic tracking correction without manual adjustment.</p>
<h2 id="tension-heading">Belt Tensioning</h2>
<p>Proper belt tension ensures the belt grips the drive pulley without slipping while minimizing wear on bearings, belt, and pulleys. Under-tensioned belts slip on the drive pulley, causing erratic speed, heat buildup, and belt wear. Over-tensioned belts overload bearings, stretch the belt, and accelerate component wear.</p>
<h3>Screw Take-Up</h3>
<p>Screw take-ups use threaded rods at the tail pulley to slide the pulley shaft forward or backward, adjusting belt tension. The operator turns the take-up screws to increase or decrease the distance between the head and tail pulleys. Screw take-ups are the most common tensioning method on shorter conveyors. Adjustment is manual and periodic, check belt tension during routine maintenance.</p>
<h3>Spring and Gravity Take-Up</h3>
<p>Spring take-ups use compression springs to maintain constant belt tension automatically. As the belt stretches with use, the springs compensate by pushing the tail pulley outward. Gravity take-ups use a weighted carriage that maintains tension through gravity force. Both methods provide automatic tensioning that reduces maintenance frequency. They suit longer conveyors and applications where consistent tension matters for product handling precision.</p>
<h3>Tension Indicators</h3>
<p>Belt slip is the primary indicator of under-tension: the belt moves slower than the drive pulley, products accumulate at the infeed, and the drive pulley may generate heat and noise. Belt sag between carry rollers (roller bed) or belt camber (bowing to one side) indicates tension issues. Excessive bearing temperature indicates over-tension. A properly tensioned belt has just enough tension to prevent slipping under maximum load, no more.</p>
<h2 id="belt-types-heading">Belt Material Selection</h2>
<p><strong>PVC belting:</strong> The most common belt material for general packaging. PVC belts suit moderate temperatures, resist moisture and mild chemicals, and come in smooth, textured, or grip-top surfaces. Standard PVC belts suit case handling, carton transport, and general <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line</a> applications.</p>
<p><strong>Polyurethane belting:</strong> Polyurethane belts provide higher abrasion resistance, better cut resistance, and wider chemical compatibility than PVC. They suit rough products, sharp-edged items, and environments with chemical exposure. Polyurethane costs more than PVC but lasts longer under demanding conditions.</p>
<p><strong>Silicone belting:</strong> Silicone belts handle higher temperatures than PVC or polyurethane and are available in food-contact grades. Check the belt maker's rated temperature range, and ask for a compliance statement for direct food contact (rubber articles for repeated food contact fall under FDA's <a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/cdrh/cfdocs/cfcfr/CFRSearch.cfm?fr=177.2600" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR 177.2600</a>). They serve bakery conveyors, oven discharge, and <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food packaging</a> where direct product contact requires food-grade material at elevated temperatures.</p>
<p><strong>Modular plastic belting:</strong> Modular belts use interlocking plastic segments instead of a continuous flat belt. Modular belts handle curves, inclines, and harsh washdown environments. Individual damaged segments replace without replacing the entire belt. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/chain-conveyors/">chain conveyor guide</a> for table-top chain (a form of modular belting).</p>
<p><strong>Incline belting:</strong> Incline belt conveyors use belts with textured surfaces (rough-top, chevron, cleated) to prevent products from sliding backward on inclines. Rough-top belts handle moderate inclines for flat-bottom products. Cleated belts handle steeper inclines and loose products. The workable angle depends on product weight, center of gravity, and bottom surface, so confirm it with a product test. Incline belt conveyors connect floor-level operations to elevated <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems</a>, mezzanines, and overhead conveyors.</p>
