<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a flat belt conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Flat bed conveyor is the common name for a flat belt conveyor: a continuous flat belt on head and tail pulleys carrying products on its upper surface. Slider bed (belt on a pan) for light loads and short runs. Roller bed (belt on rollers) for heavier loads and longer runs. One of the most versatile conveyor types. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/belt-conveyors/">belt conveyor applications</a> guide for all belt sub-types.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">Slider bed vs roller bed, which do I need?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Slider bed for light to moderate loads, small or unstable products, and knife-edge transfers. Roller bed for heavier loads and lower belt friction. Example ratings: Hytrol Model SB 100 lbs per foot, Model RB 225 lbs per foot. Most packaging applications use slider bed for small products and roller bed for case handling.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How do I track and tension a belt conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Tracking: adjust tail pulley screws in small increments and let the belt run several revolutions between adjustments. V-guides and crowned pulleys provide automatic centering. Tensioning: screw take-ups for manual adjustment, spring/gravity take-ups for automatic. Proper tension prevents slipping without overloading bearings.</p>

</div>
</div>
